, B6-B8 (678), not matching 123/456/789 exactly \u2014 so no.  \n\n**Scoring potential:**  \nIf we discard F2 \u2192 all simples (2) + all chows (2) = 4 points \u2192 not enough 8. Need more.  \n\nMaybe **Half Flush** \u2014 discard B6 and F2, keep only T suit.  \nBut we have W and B melds already exposed \u2192 can't change melds, so Half Flush impossible.  \n\nGiven exposed melds are W and B, we cannot get Half Flush or Full Flush.  \n\nSo maybe aim for **Mixed Straight** after all? Wait, W5-W7 is 567 in W, B6-B8 is 678 in B, then if we make T4-T6 chow in hand? We have T4 T5 T5 B6 \u2014 missing T6. T6 is dead? Unshown tiles: T6: 2 left, possible. Then Mixed Straight? No, 567(W), 678(B), 456(T) \u2014 not 123/456/789 triple.  \n\n**Check All Types** (6 points): Need all 3 suits + wind + dragon. We have W, B, T suits in melds/hand, wind (F2), but no dragon (J). J1 is 0 left, J2/J3 possible but not in hand. So not yet.  \n\nGiven the difficulty reaching 8 points, maybe just maximize tile efficiency to win first, then check patterns:  \n\nHand after forming chow T2 T3 T4:  \nUse T2 T3 T4 as chow, left: T2, T5, T5, B6, F2 \u2192 need one more chow and pair. Could be:  \n- Chow T4 T5 T6 (but T4 used) \u2014 no.  \n- Chow T3 T4 T5 (T3, T4 used) \u2014 no.  \nSo maybe T2 T3 T4 chow not best.  \n\nTry: keep T2 T2 T3 T4 T5 T5 B6 F2 \u2014 possible melds:  \n- Chow T2 T3 T4 (use T2, T3, T4)  \n- Chow T4 T5 T6 (use T4, T5, B6) but T4 already used.  \n\nBetter: treat T5 T5 as pair, need two chows from remaining: T2 T2 T3 T4 B6 F2.  \nPossible chow: T2 T3 T4 (use T2 T3 T4) leaves T2 B6 F2 \u2014 useless.  \n\nMaybe discard F2 first to reduce honors, try for All Simples + All Chows + Short Straight etc.  \n\nLet's check valid actions: Play F2, T2, T5, B6, T4, T3.  \n\nDiscard F2: no honors left \u2192 All Simples (2) possible.  \nDiscard B6: keep F2 \u2192 no All Simples.  \n\nWhich is safer?  \nUnshown tiles: F2: 4 left, so safe. B6: 3 left, relatively safe.  \nBut discarding B6 loses possible chow B4-B6 or B6-B8 (B8 in meld).  \n\nBetter to discard F2 now to aim for All Simples + All Chows + maybe Short Straight.  \n\n**Final choice:** Discard F2 first for safety and pattern potential.  \n\n**Evaluation:** F2 is a safe discard (many left), removes honor to possibly score All Simples, and keeps flexibility in T suit for chows.  \n\n**Reason:** F2 is not needed for any high-value pattern given current exposed melds; keeping it blocks All Simples. Discarding it reduces risk (4 copies unseen) and aligns with going for All Chows + All Simples + possibly Short Straight or Two Terminal Chows if we later form T1-T3 or T7-T9 chow.  \n\n**Answer:** Play F2"}
